#lang at-exp racket/base

(require ycao-net/lib/markup)

(define post
  @article["Thoughts After Playing Outer Wilds" "2025-09-02"]{
    @blockquote{
        The following content contains spoilers for the plot of Outer Wilds.
    }
    @para{
        Outer Wilds is a hymn to explorers, delivered through exploration.
    }
    @para{
        Since the beginning, our human ancestors have naturally been curious about distant places. Curiosity about 'over there' drove us out of the African continent and to spread across the Earth. Outer Wilds speaks directly to this innate human curiosity. The game has no objectives; there is only a little guidance from the observatory at the beginning, and the rest of the path must be walked by the player themselves by following one unknown phenomenon after another. At first, the player is eager to know what the masks are; then the scattered ruins on the ground attract the player's interest; and the fragmentary information between the lines on Nomai artifacts draws the player deeper. 'I found it!'—this ancient delight constantly drives the player and gives rise to all kinds of different experiences.
    }
    @para{
        In Outer Wilds, there is no growth in the traditional sense: the player does not acquire new items or achieve higher stats. At the end of each loop, the only thing that changes is the knowledge the player has mastered. But the magic is that across loop after loop, this knowledge genuinely brings change. The player gradually comes to understand the past and grasps the laws of the universe. What the player needs is not a stat or a key, but simply a piece of information. Everything—simple or complex, even the structures that can help the universe escape the loop—has been lying there ever since the first time the player opens their eyes at the campfire, waiting for the player to uncover it through a specific sequence of actions.
    }
    @para{
        Many games dress puzzles in narrative clothing, while Outer Wilds uses puzzles to narrate. The game's climax occurs when its fragmented story threads come together to paint a tragic and heroic epic. My most striking experience was at the Orbital Probe Tracking Station: the computer reveals that, to circumvent the (obvious) limitation that the supernova only erupts once, the Nomai civilization used the time loop to preserve data; by repeatedly probing at random more than 9M times, they traversed the entire star system's space and found the Eye of the Universe. At the start of each loop, as soon as the protagonist opens their eyes, they can see the probe being fired randomly into deep space; this is in fact the Nomai, hundreds of thousands of years later, still painstakingly searching for an answer. At several key moments, the player can experience the so-called Aha Moment. Because all the clues have been pieced together by the player through immense difficulty, these 'sudden realizations' deliver an incomparably powerful impact.
    }
    @para{
        The game's ending brings this epic to a satisfying and philosophical conclusion: holding the advanced warp core—the crowning achievement of Nomai technology—I risk ending the solar system and restart the abandoned ship. The main theme of Final Voyage is the same as the end-of-loop music, but with an extra frequency, telling of the Nomai's desperate race, which lasted for hundreds of thousands of years yet was unexpectedly stopped; the last puzzle piece is fitted into place by the player, also hinting that 'this time is different'. The Eye conjures a museum and a forest, within which the old universe moves toward heat death; only a moment earlier the Nomai skeletons were still gazing at the starry sky, and in the blink of an eye the ship races into the unknown; the quantum-state explorers gather together and, with one final song, sketch out a new universe. Only through the perseverance and sacrifice of generation after generation of explorers has modern civilization been lifted on the shoulders of giants to such heights.
    }
    @para{
        Outer Wilds has one and only one gameplay mode: exploration. But that is enough, because exploration is humanity's oldest and most precious instinct.
    }
  })

(provide post)